From: PlayThing builder <builder@localhost>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] dma-buf/sw_sync: remove disable_signaling

This tree has the rb-tree version of sw_sync (Linux 4.14), but it kept the
disable_signaling callback of the old version. That callback removes a sync_pt
from pt_list, but not from pt_tree. Two failures follow:

- The timeline signals only the points in pt_list, so the fence does not
  signal any more, and a later waiter waits until its timeout.
- timeline_fence_release() removes a point from pt_tree only when the point is
  in pt_list. Thus the freed point stays in pt_tree, and the next
  sync_pt_create() reads freed memory. The OSD driver (osd_timeline_0) makes
  its out-fences on this timeline: a restart of SurfaceFlinger closes polled,
  unsignaled out-fences and caused "Unable to handle kernel paging request" in
  sync_pt_create() from osd_ioctl(), then a kernel panic.

Linux 4.14 and later have no disable_signaling for sw_sync; do the same. A
point then stays in pt_list until it signals or until its release removes it
from the list and the tree.
---
--- a/drivers/dma-buf/sw_sync.c
+++ b/drivers/dma-buf/sw_sync.c
@@ -169,13 +169,6 @@
 	return true;
 }
 
-static void timeline_fence_disable_signaling(struct fence *fence)
-{
-	struct sync_pt *pt = container_of(fence, struct sync_pt, base);
-
-	list_del_init(&pt->link);
-}
-
 static void timeline_fence_value_str(struct fence *fence,
 				    char *str, int size)
 {
@@ -194,7 +187,6 @@
 	.get_driver_name = timeline_fence_get_driver_name,
 	.get_timeline_name = timeline_fence_get_timeline_name,
 	.enable_signaling = timeline_fence_enable_signaling,
-	.disable_signaling = timeline_fence_disable_signaling,
 	.signaled = timeline_fence_signaled,
 	.wait = fence_default_wait,
 	.release = timeline_fence_release,
